2: eno2: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 qdisc fq_codel state UP group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 30:d0:42:fc:44:5a brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
    altname enp0s31f6
    inet 10.15.201.1/16 metric 100 brd 10.15.255.255 scope global eno2
       valid_lft forever preferred_lft forever
    inet6 fe80::32d0:42ff:fefc:445a/64 scope link
       valid_lft forever preferred_lft forever
3: wlo1: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> mtu 1500 qdisc noop state DOWN group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 00:93:37:40:0e:8e brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
    altname wlp0s20f3
4: docker0: <NO-CARRIER,BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP> mtu 1500 qdisc noqueue state DOWN group default
    link/ether 02:42:3b:e4:15:a0 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
    inet 172.17.0.1/16 brd 172.17.255.255 scope global docker0
       valid_lft forever preferred_lft forever
```

So my ip address is the one in eno2. Since I am in a virtual machine and this could change I will set the ip address in the Makefile. 
With this command it is easy to get the ip address and pass it to the docker container:
```bash
# Function to get the IP address
get_ip = $(shell hostname -I | cut -d' ' -f1)
```
I will pass it to the container by adding the following to the  Makefile:  
```bash
cd srcs && IP_ADDR=$(get_ip) docker compose up  --remove-orphans --build
```

And to simplify the login to the ftp server I can add the following command to the makefile:
```bash
# to connect to the ftp server from the makefile
ftp: 	
	ftp $(get_ip)
```


## connecting to my ftp server container to debug
You can use the `docker exec` command to access the running FTP container and inspect the files. Here is how you can do it:

1. **List Running Containers**: First, list all running containers to get the container ID or name of your FTP container.

```sh
docker ps
```

2. **Exec into the FTP Container**: Use the container ID or name to exec into the FTP container.

```sh
docker exec -it <container_id_or_name> /bin/sh
```

For example, if your FTP container is named `inception_ftp_1`, you would run:

```sh
docker exec -it inception_ftp_1 /bin/sh
```

3. **Navigate to the Directory**: Once inside the container, navigate to the directory where the files are located.

```sh
cd /var/www/html
ls
```

This will allow you to see the files inside the FTP container.

